  <content>
    <description>
      <levelledPara>
        <title>Summary</title>
        <para>The four aluminium-lithium corner fittings of the forward cargo door cut-out are replaced by machined titanium items with a thicker flange and a larger load introduction radius, raising the fatigue allowable under the high-density freight spectrum.</para>
      </levelledPara>
      <levelledPara>
        <title>Reason</title>
        <para>Re-substantiation of the forward cargo door surround against the high-density freight loading spectrum introduced for combi and heavy-cargo operators showed a reserve factor shortfall at the lower corner fittings. Crack initiation was predicted between 34 000 and 42 000 flight cycles against a design service goal well above that. No in-service finding existed at launch; the change was driven entirely by the revised spectrum and by the fleet leader projection presented at the 2017 structures review.</para>
        <figure>
          <title>Fuselage Section 13 — Forward — Door Surround Structure — location, side view</title>
          <graphic infoEntityIdent="ICN-MRD-A-532113-A-U8025-365B809801-A-001-01" />
        </figure>
      </levelledPara>
      <levelledPara>
        <title>Description</title>
        <para>The four aluminium-lithium fittings at the lower forward and lower aft corners of the cargo door cut-out are replaced by machined Ti-6Al-4V items of the same external envelope but with a 2.4 mm thicker attachment flange and a 9 mm fillet radius at the load introduction point. The web assembly D5321003375416 is retained and is re-drilled locally to the new pattern; the joint is closed with titanium bolts AES495354-07 and countersunk washers AES963502K7 in place of the previous protruding-head set. Revised detail is carried on drawings D53211308437 and D53211167819. Access is through the forward cargo compartment in zones 113 and 115, with the lower shell lining removed locally.</para>
        <para>Neither the door itself nor the door actuation installation is touched.</para>
      </levelledPara>
      <levelledPara>
        <title>Effects</title>
        <para>Mass increase of 3.1 kg per aircraft, symmetric about the centre line, with a centre-of-gravity shift below the reporting threshold. No electrical load change. The fittings are not interchangeable with the previous standard because the fastener pattern differs, and a mixed installation on one door surround is not permitted. Embodiment removes the 3 000 flight cycle repeat detailed visual inspection introduced by the original substantiation, which is the main operating benefit.</para>
        <para>Illustrated parts content for ATA 53-21 and the associated removal task require reissue. The substantiation method behind this change — a load-path redistribution argument taken at the lower corner rather than a gross-area allowable — has since been read across to the freighter main deck cargo door case of MOD-160560. Nothing in the parts list, the fastener standard or the drawing set is common between the two, and the read-across is of the method only.</para>
      </levelledPara>
      <levelledPara>
        <title>Embodiment note</title>
        <para>Cut in on the production line at MSN M00300 in August 2020. Delivered aircraft are brought to the standard through Service Bulletin MRD-53-1012, which is preceded by the one-off inspection of Service Bulletin MRD-53-1008. Kits are held as spares under the same reference and typical embodiment is four days inside a scheduled C-check. The design justification now carries the read-across decision to MOD-160560 as a recorded item, so that the freighter substantiation is not re-argued from first principles at the next revision of either dossier.</para>
      </levelledPara>
